<span id="id41.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id41.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id41.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">José Duato</span></span>
<span id="id41.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id41.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id41.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Openchip &amp; Software Technologies</span></span></span>
<span id="id41.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id41.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Barcelona, Spain</span></span>
<span id="id41.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id41.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">jose.duato@openchip.es</span></span>
</span>
</span>
<span id="id42" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:113.9pt;">

<span id="id42.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id42.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id42.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Enrique S. Quintana-Ortí</span></span>
<span id="id42.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id42.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id42.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Universitat Politècnica de València</span></span></span>
<span id="id42.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id42.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Valencia, Spain</span></span>
<span id="id42.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id42.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">quintana@disca.upv.es</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span><span id="id43" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>*Corresponding author</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Federated learning (FL) enables collaborative training across organizations without sharing raw data, but it is hindered by statistical heterogeneity (non-i.i.d. client data) and by instability of naive weight averaging under client drift. In many cross-silo deployments, FL is warm-started from a strong pretrained backbone (e.g., ImageNet-1K) and then adapted to local domains. Motivated by recent evidence that ReLU-like gating regimes (structural knowledge) stabilize earlier than the remaining parameter values (quantitative knowledge), we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> (Federated Structural-Quantitative learning), a transfer-initialized neural federated procedure based on a DualCopy, piecewise-linear view of deep networks. <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> freezes a structural copy of the pretrained model to induce fixed binary gating masks during federated fine-tuning, while only a quantitative copy is optimized locally and aggregated across rounds. Fixing the gating reduces learning to within-regime affine refinements, which stabilizes aggregation under heterogeneous partitions. Experiments on two convolutional neural network backbones under i.i.d. and Dirichlet splits show that <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> improves robustness and can reduce rounds-to-best validation performance relative to standard baselines while preserving accuracy in the transfer setting.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
Federated learning, cross-silo, transfer learning, ImageNet pretraining, activation patterns, communication efficiency

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proliferation of connected devices and the digitization of organizational processes have led to an unprecedented amount of data being generated in distributed settings. In many real-world scenarios, collecting such data in a centralized repository is impractical or undesirable due to privacy, legal, or operational constraints. In this context,  <a href="#id7"><span href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</span></a> (<a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a>) has emerged as a paradigm to collaboratively train  <a href="#id12"><span href="#id12" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ml</span></a> (<a href="#id12"><abbr href="#id12" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ml</abbr></a>) and  <a href="#id5"><span href="#id5" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">dl</span></a> (<a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">dl</abbr></a>) models while keeping raw data local to each participant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its appeal, <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> introduces fundamental challenges that are absent or less pronounced in centralized training. First, client data is often statistically heterogeneous (non-i.i.d.), which can lead to unstable or slow convergence and to suboptimal global models when using standard federated averaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. This heterogeneity may stem from label distribution shifts, feature shifts, or domain shifts across clients, motivating a large body of work aimed at improving robustness under non-i.i.d. data. Representative examples include approaches based on local normalization to address feature shift (<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedBN</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, methods that exploit shared representations with personalized local components (<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedRep</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and architectures that explicitly account for heterogeneity through specialization, such as federated mixtures of experts (<span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedMix</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, communication between server and clients frecuently becomes a dominant bottleneck, particularly when models are large or bandwidth is limited. Beyond reducing the number of communication rounds by increasing local computation (as in federated averaging), a complementary line of work targets communication efficiency through structured updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, gradient quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, aggressive sparsification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, or compression schemes tailored to federated non-i.i.d. regimes (e.g., STC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. These methods highlight a central trade-off in <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a>: improving statistical efficiency (accuracy and convergence) while minimizing system costs (communication, latency, and client-side computation).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A practical aspect often overlooked in methodological discussions is that many real deployments of <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> do not start from random initialization. Instead, it is common to warm-start federated training from a backbone pre-trained on large-scale public data (e.g., ImageNet-1K in computer vision) and then perform federated fine-tuning on the private client datasets. Recent systematic studies show that such pre-trained initialization can reduce the number of communication rounds required to reach a target accuracy and can dampen the adverse effects of data and system heterogeneity in federated optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. This practice is also consistent with classical  <a href="#id31"><span href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</span></a> (<a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a>) evidence that early representations learned on large visual corpora tend to be broadly reusable across downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work suggests that learning dynamics in ReLU-based <a href="#id6"><span href="#id6" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">dnn</span></a> can be fruitfully understood through two complementary components:  <a href="#id30"><span href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</span></a> (<a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a>) and  <a href="#id24"><span href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</span></a> (<a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a>). <a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a> corresponds to the gating behaviour induced by ReLU nonlinearities, i.e., the activation masks (activation patterns) that select active paths and thus, the piecewise-affine region in which the network operates for a given input. <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> corresponds to the numerical values of weights and biases that determine the affine mapping within those active regions. Empirical evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> suggests that activation-pattern changes decay substantially earlier than weight-update magnitudes across multiple architectures, motivating a two-phase interpretation of training. Along the same lines, Duato et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> formalize the <a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a>/<a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> separation and demonstrate that freezing the structural component while retraining only <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> can accelerate (re-)training, with direct implications for incremental and federated re-training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by these findings, we propose <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span>, where all participants start from the same ImageNet-1K pretrained initialization, which acts as the fixed structural component, while clients communicate and aggregate only <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> updates. This induces a late-stage specialization in which optimization proceeds largely within stable activation regimes, reducing client-side compute and improving performance per round in the federated stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, in this paper we make the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span>, a transfer-initialized two-stage <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> procedure that decouples <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">structural</em> (gating) from <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantitative</em> (weights) knowledge for cross-silo settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a federated protocol that freezes the structural component and aggregates only quantitative updates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically evaluate <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> against standard federated baselines under heterogeneous client data and report improvements in convergence efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> trains models across distributed data holders without moving raw data, typically via iterative rounds of local optimization followed by server-side aggregation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In practical deployments, cross-silo <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> involves a small number of stable clients with reliable connectivity, while cross-device <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> targets massive numbers of intermittently available devices; these regimes differ in their dominant bottlenecks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. A core difficulty is that client data are typically non-i.i.d., yielding slow or unstable convergence under vanilla <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedAvg</span> due to <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">client drift</em>. <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedProx</span> mitigates client drift with a proximal term discouraging excessive deviation from the global iterate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Adaptive server-side optimization (<span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedOpt</span>, including <span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedAdam</span>/<span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedYogi</span>) improves robustness and tuning under heterogeneous data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Beyond label skew, non-i.i.d. feature distributions can cause mismatched batch-normalization statistics and degrade aggregation quality; <span id="S2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedBN</span> addresses this by keeping batch normalization statistics local <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Another branch targets personalization by learning shared representations with client-specific components, such as <span id="S2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedRep</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> or <span id="S2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedBABU</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> is the de facto starting point in many cross-silo vision deployments: models are initialized from pretrained backbones and then adapted to downstream domains. The transferability of deep representations has been extensively studied; notably, some studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> quantify how transferability decreases from early to late layers and identify co-adaptation as a practical obstacle. These motivates our design choice to <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> initialize from a strong pretrained model and <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> constrain the subsequent federated adaptation to the subset of parameters that most benefit from domain-specific refinement, which appears to mitigates the umbalanced and drift effect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our method relates to an emerging perspective that many <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">dnn</abbr></a> can be viewed through a <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gating</em> lens, where a discrete routing/selection mechanism determines which computational substructures are active, and the remaining parameters refine the mapping within those selected regimes. While the original <a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a>/<a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> decomposition in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is articulated for ReLU architectures (where the gate is naturally binary), <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> does not assume ReLU nor any specific activation in the pretrained backbone. Instead, we explicitly introduce a <em id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">binary</em> gating surrogate, enforcing a hard (ReLU-like) activation mask during the federated stage, regardless of the activation used during pretraining. This yields a well-defined separation between <a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a> (the induced binary gating patterns) and <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> (the parameters optimized within those fixed patterns), allowing us to freeze the former and federate only the latter to enable efficient specialization under heterogeneous client data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method proposal</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Background</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus primarily on <a href="#id4"><span href="#id4" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">cnn</span></a>, as they are among the most widely adopted architectures in <a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">dl</abbr></a>. To formalize the setting of our proposal, we first introduce the following two definitions, which provide the necessary background on <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">cnn</abbr></a> and activation patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">cnn</abbr></a> is a  <a href="#id8"><span href="#id8" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ff</span></a> (<a href="#id8"><abbr href="#id8" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ff</abbr></a>) that maps an input image tensor
<math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{C_{0}\times H_{0}\times W_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{C_{0}\times H_{0}\times W_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> to an output <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;w)</annotation></semantics></math> by applying a sequence of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> convolutional blocks followed by a head. Given <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}\coloneqq x," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}\coloneqq x,</annotation></semantics></math> we have:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\qquad z_{\ell}=\mathrm{Conv}_{\ell}(h_{\ell-1};w_{\ell})\qquad h_{\ell}\;=\;\phi_{\ell}\!\big(z_{\ell}\big),\quad\ell=1,\dots,L," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\qquad z_{\ell}=\mathrm{Conv}_{\ell}(h_{\ell-1};w_{\ell})\qquad h_{\ell}\;=\;\phi_{\ell}\!\big(z_{\ell}\big),\quad\ell=1,\dots,L,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;w)\;=\;g(h_{L};w_{\mathrm{out}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;w)\;=\;g(h_{L};w_{\mathrm{out}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Conv}_{\ell}(\cdot;w_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Conv}_{\ell}(\cdot;w_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a convolutional layer (with its weights <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the activation function (and possibly batch normalization/pooling/residual addition), <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds with the preactivation of layer <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the input to layer <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the final classifier/regressor head. The full parameter set is <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{L},w_{\mathrm{out}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\{w_{1},\dots,w_{L},w_{\mathrm{out}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">cnn</abbr></a> with <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> convolutional blocks that include a pointwise nonlinearity. For an input <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{C_{0}\times H_{0}\times W_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{C_{0}\times H_{0}\times W_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\ell}(x;w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\ell}(x;w)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pre-activation tensor of block <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> (the tensor right before the nonlinearity is applied). We define the <em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">activation mask</em> of block <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}(x;w)\;\coloneqq\;\mathds{1}\!\big[z_{\ell}(x;w)&gt;0\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.108em">≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}(x;w)\;\coloneqq\;\mathds{1}\!\big[z_{\ell}(x;w)&gt;0\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟙</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the characteristic function, and the inequality is applied elementwise, so <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}(x;w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}(x;w)</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary tensor with the same shape as <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\ell}(x;w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\ell}(x;w)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">activation pattern</em> induced by <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the collection of all masks across blocks:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(x;w)\;\coloneqq\;\big(\alpha_{1}(x;w),\dots,\alpha_{L}(x;w)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(x;w)\;\coloneqq\;\big(\alpha_{1}(x;w),\dots,\alpha_{L}(x;w)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Federated algorithm proposal</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>), the goal is to minimize</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(w)\;=\;\sum_{i=1}^{M}\frac{n_{i}}{n}\,\mathcal{L}_{i}(w),\qquad\text{where }n=\sum_{i=1}^{M}n_{i};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(w)\;=\;\sum_{i=1}^{M}\frac{n_{i}}{n}\,\mathcal{L}_{i}(w),\qquad\text{where }n=\sum_{i=1}^{M}n_{i};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of clients, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples held by client <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of samples across all clients. In addition, the local objective is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}(w)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}_{i}}\big[\,\ell_{i}(w;x)\,\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}(w)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}_{i}}\big[\,\ell_{i}(w;x)\,\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the data distribution of client <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}(w;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}(w;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-sample loss (e.g., cross-entropy) evaluated by the shared parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> on sample <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
We adopt the standard assumptions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> for nonconvex optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In a standard synchronous federated round <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the server samples a subset of clients and broadcasts the current global parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each selected client <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> then performs several local optimization steps on its private data, producing either an updated set of parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t+1,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t+1,i}</annotation></semantics></math> or an equivalent update <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta w_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta w_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The server aggregates these client contributions to obtain the next global iterate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then broadcasted to clients for the next round <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> denote the model to be federated, with parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the DualCopy construction of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, we rewrite the model as a duplicated-parameter approximation with two disjoint copies,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{w}\;=\;\big(w^{\mathrm{SK}},\,w^{\mathrm{QK}}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{w}\;=\;\big(w^{\mathrm{SK}},\,w^{\mathrm{QK}}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, from a certain point on we duplicate <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which encodes the <em id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">structural</em> component that induces (binary) gating regimes; and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which encodes the <em id="S3.SS2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantitative</em> component of the within-regime mappings. Although this construction doubles the stored parameters, it preserves the number of trainable parameters in our procedure as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> is kept frozen while <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized and aggregated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All participants (server and clients) initialize from a shared pretrained checkpoint <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This warm-start is a key departure from training-from-scratch <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a>: the federation begins from a representation that already captures generic visual structure, and subsequent training focuses on efficient downstream adaptation. In our setting, the pretrained initialization instantiates the structural component, i.e., we set <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}\leftarrow w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}\leftarrow w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math> at initialization. Given an input <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{C_{0}\times H_{0}\times W_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{C_{0}\times H_{0}\times W_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the structural copy <a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a> produces pre-activations
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{SK}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>SK</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{SK}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and induces a fixed binary mask
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}(x)\coloneqq\mathds{1}[z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{SK}}(x)&gt;0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>SK</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}(x)\coloneqq\mathds{1}[z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{SK}}(x)&gt;0]</annotation></semantics></math>.
During the federated stage, the quantitative copy computes
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{QK}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>QK</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{QK}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and applies the fixed gate:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\ell}(x)\;=\;\alpha_{\ell}(x)\odot z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{QK}}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>QK</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\ell}(x)\;=\;\alpha_{\ell}(x)\odot z_{\ell}^{\mathrm{QK}}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the elementwise product. Conditioned on <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{\ell}(x)\}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{\ell}(x)\}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, the network reduces to a within-region affine map, where the network behaves as a linear transformation plus bias within the corresponding region <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Hence, freezing <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> amounts to fixing the per-sample routing mechanism, while federated training updates only the within-region affine parameters encoded by <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This knowledge separation is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ III-B Federated algorithm proposal ‣ III Method proposal ‣ FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02990v1/diagram.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/142;" width="476" height="142" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Decoupling view of a network into <a href="#id30"><abbr href="#id30" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sk</abbr></a> representing the values of the activations (left), represented by activation masks (gating); and <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a>, represented by the affine parameters (edge coefficients), with its activation patterns frozen (right). In <span id="S3.F1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span>, models are initialized from a common pretrained checkpoint and then adapted federatively by updating only <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> after structural stabilization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a calibration set which can be public, synthetic, or obtained via privacy-preserving transformations of client data. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathcal{D}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathcal{D}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed probe subset used for evaluation. In this paper, we warm-start from pretrained weights, which can be interpreted as already providing a structurally mature initialization. We propose a centralized phase used to select an effective <a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> strategy for the federation. Specifically, starting from the shared pretrained checkpoint <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the server performs a lightweight calibration step on <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>: it does not attempt to train the final federated model, but instead evaluates candidate <a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> schedules (which layers to freeze/unfreeze and in what order) and selects the schedule that yields the most stable and effective adaptation from <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the probe set <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The selected schedule is then broadcast to all clients, which follow it during federated training to enforce a consistent adaptation protocol across silos and to mitigate catastrophic forgetting of the pretrained representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">After this, we freeze the structural component <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> and run standard synchronous <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">fl</abbr></a> rounds in which clients update only <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math> on their private data. Concretely, during local training each client uses the frozen <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute binary gating masks and optimizes <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math> within these fixed activation regimes. Server aggregation is performed on <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math> only, following the same schema as <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedAvg</span>.
Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-B Federated algorithm proposal ‣ III Method proposal ‣ FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the full procedure. Lines 2-6 correspond to the <a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> regime: the server evaluates candidate <a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> schedules, selects the best-performing one, broadcasts it to all clients, and instantiates the structural component with the pretrained weights. Once <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined, the server broadcasts it to all clients and the federated stage commences. At each round, the server samples a subset of clients (line 8) and broadcasts the current quantitative parameters. Each selected client then trains locally for a fixed number of epochs on its private data, keeping <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> frozen and updating only <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the selected schedule. Finally, the server aggregates the received quantitative parameters (line 15) to form the next global quantitative iterate. This federated loop (lines 7–15) is repeated for <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> communication rounds.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span>: transfer-initialized calibration of <a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> schedule + federated <a href="#id24"><abbr href="#id24" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">qk</abbr></a> optimization</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> clients <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math> with local datasets <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{D}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{D}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and sizes <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{n_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{n_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathcal{D}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathcal{D}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> subset;
pretrained checkpoint <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math>; local epochs <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>; total rounds <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>; sampled clients per round <math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\leftarrow\mathrm{ObtainSchedule}\!\left(w^{\mathrm{PT}},\,S\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:6.906em;" width="6.906em"><mi>ObtainSchedule</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\leftarrow\mathrm{ObtainSchedule}\!\left(w^{\mathrm{PT}},\,S\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
             Broadcast training schedule <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to all clients.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}\leftarrow w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}\leftarrow w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}\leftarrow w^{\mathrm{PT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>PT</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}\leftarrow w^{\mathrm{PT}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
             Freeze <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{SK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{SK}}</annotation></semantics></math> and broadcast it once to all clients.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
             <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,\ldots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,\ldots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              Server samples <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq M</annotation></semantics></math> clients and broadcasts <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>QK</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each client <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">in parallel</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
               Client follows schedule <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and updates <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">only</span> <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>QK</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
               Client computes binary gating masks <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{\ell}(x;w^{\mathrm{SK}})\}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>SK</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{\ell}(x;w^{\mathrm{SK}})\}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
               Client trains for <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> local epochs on <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining <math id="alg1.l12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t+1,i}^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>QK</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t+1,i}^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
               Client sends <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t+1,i}^{\mathrm{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>QK</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t+1,i}^{\mathrm{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the server.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              Server aggregates QK:

<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t+1}^{\mathrm{QK}}\;=\;\sum_{i\in\mathcal{K}_{t}}\frac{n_{i}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{K}_{t}}n_{j}}\,w_{t+1,i}^{\mathrm{QK}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>QK</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>QK</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t+1}^{\mathrm{QK}}\;=\;\sum_{i\in\mathcal{K}_{t}}\frac{n_{i}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{K}_{t}}n_{j}}\,w_{t+1,i}^{\mathrm{QK}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
             <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary objective of this evaluation is to assess the performance of <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> against standard federated aggregation schemes under identical experimental conditions, including <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> a common pretrained initialization; and <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> a centrally selected <a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> schedule when applicable. Accordingly, our hyperparameter choices are not intended to reflect an optimal or realistic federated tuning procedure, but rather to establish a strong and stable baseline so that performance differences can be attributed to the aggregation strategy itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we consider two representative <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">cnn</abbr></a>: <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AlexNet</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, due to its simplicity and importance in the <a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">dl</abbr></a> community, and <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ResNet18</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, due to its efficiency and wide adoption in computer vision.
<span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AlexNet</span> is trained on CINIC-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, a proxy based on CIFAR-10 with ImageNet-1K images usage, while <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ResNet18</span> is trained on CIFAR-100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, a more realistic cross-silo use case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were conducted using Python v3.10, PyTorch v2.6 and Flower<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
          https://flower.ai/</span></span></span> v1.25. To ensure reproducibility, random seeds were fixed across all relevant libraries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Hyperparameters were chosen to maximize validation accuracy for the baseline method and then kept fixed across all methods within each model/dataset suite. Concretely, we ran preliminary ablations over a short horizon of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> epochs on a grid of learning rate (LR) and weight decay (WD),
<math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathrm{LR},\mathrm{WD})\in\{10^{-1},\,10^{-2},\,10^{-3}\}\times\{10^{-3},\,10^{-4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>LR</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>WD</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn> 10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn> 10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn> 10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{LR},\mathrm{WD})\in\{10^{-1},\,10^{-2},\,10^{-3}\}\times\{10^{-3},\,10^{-4}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and selected <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathrm{LR},\mathrm{WD})=(10^{-2},\,10^{-4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>LR</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>WD</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn> 10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{LR},\mathrm{WD})=(10^{-2},\,10^{-4})</annotation></semantics></math> as it yielded the highest validation accuracy. Unless otherwise stated, all experiments use batch size <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> per client.
We emphasize that such centralized hyperparameter search may be impractical in fully realistic federated deployments; here it is used solely to ensure a competitive and consistent baseline for comparing aggregation procedures. We also studied results under both <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SGD</span> and <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AdamW</span>, using the same selected <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathrm{LR},\mathrm{WD})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>LR</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>WD</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{LR},\mathrm{WD})</annotation></semantics></math> pair for fair comparison across aggregation schemes, finally keeping SGD due to better results on the baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#id31"><abbr href="#id31" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">tl</abbr></a> schedules are selected centrally as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Federated algorithm proposal ‣ III Method proposal ‣ FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a> using a calibration protocol. In particular, we determine the freezing depth by starting from a conservative policy (freezing up to the penultimate block, i.e., training only the last two blocks) and progressively unfreezing earlier blocks until validation accuracy no longer improves (or starts decreasing), which we interpret as a signal of incipient catastrophic forgetting. Under this criterion, for <span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AlexNet</span> we only train the classifier, whereas for <span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ResNet18</span> we train both the <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>4</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> convolutional block and the classifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We simulate a cross-silo federation with <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=10</annotation></semantics></math> clients and a single central server. We consider two client partition schemes implemented in the Flower framework: <span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> an i.i.d. split (uniform random assignment of samples); and <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> a heterogeneous Dirichlet split with concentration parameter <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. We use full participation (<math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=M</annotation></semantics></math>) and one local epoch per communication round (<math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=1</annotation></semantics></math>). For <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AlexNet</span>/CINIC-10 we run <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=5</annotation></semantics></math> rounds since the model saturates quickly (only the classifier is trained under the selected schedule). For <span id="S4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ResNet18</span>/CIFAR-100 we run <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=30</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, using as a reference the number of epochs needed to train the model centralized. We compare server-side aggregation strategies under two client-partition regimes. Under an i.i.d. partition, we benchmark the standard <span id="S4.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedAvg</span> procedure against the proposed <span id="S4.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span>. Under a heterogeneous Dirichlet partition, we compare <span id="S4.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedAvg</span> and <span id="S4.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedProx</span> (as it performs better than <span id="S4.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedAvg</span> under unbalanced clients) against <span id="S4.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span>, in order to assess robustness under client drift.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2604.02990v1/fed_sq.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/482;" width="476" height="482"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Experiments performed over different model architectures and datasets, showing an improvement of <span id="S4.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FedSQ</span> over the other approaches. Left column indicates the experiments performed over <span id="S4.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AlexNet</span>. Right column indicates the experiments performed over <span id="S4.F2.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ResNet18</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of federated algorithms. <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">bva</abbr></a> denotes the best validation score achieved during training; “Epoch” reports the communication round at which <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FedSQ:Optimized Weight Averaging via Fixed Gating" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">bva</abbr></a> is attained.</span></figcaption>
